WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.202
An AI system usually contains more than a model.

2
00:00:03.202 --> 00:00:21.936
It may include users, frontend applications, backend services, model gateways, hosted model providers, local models, prompts, system instructions, retrieved documents, vector stores, uploaded files, memory, tools, APIs, plugins, logs, queues, and downstream systems.

3
00:00:21.936 --> 00:00:28.981
The architecture should show how these pieces relate instead of treating the model as the whole product.

4
00:00:28.981 --> 00:00:37.707
Data flow mapping answers where information enters, where it is transformed, where it is stored, and where it can trigger action.

5
00:00:37.707 --> 00:00:39.308
A user may upload a file.

6
00:00:39.308 --> 00:00:43.071
A backend may enrich the request with account metadata.

7
00:00:43.071 --> 00:00:46.113
A retrieval service may add document chunks.

8
00:00:46.113 --> 00:00:48.755
A model gateway may call a hosted model.

9
00:00:48.755 --> 00:00:50.917
A tool service may invoke an API.

10
00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:53.319
A log pipeline may retain metadata.

11
00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:57.001
Each step changes exposure and control requirements.

12
00:00:57.001 --> 00:01:00.284
Authority flow is just as important as data flow.

13
00:01:00.284 --> 00:01:11.652
Authority flow shows which identity or permission is used when the system reads data, calls a tool, retrieves a document, writes memory, or changes a downstream system.

14
00:01:11.652 --> 00:01:17.256
A user may ask for something, but a backend service identity may perform the action.

15
00:01:17.256 --> 00:01:27.343
If the architecture does not show that authority transfer, teams can miss privilege escalation, shared-account risk, and unclear accountability.

16
00:01:27.343 --> 00:01:31.506
A useful diagram should make invisible assumptions visible.

17
00:01:31.506 --> 00:01:34.388
Which prompts are trusted configuration?

18
00:01:34.388 --> 00:01:37.110
Which documents are untrusted content?

19
00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.952
Which tools are read-only?

20
00:01:38.952 --> 00:01:40.953
Which tools can write or send?

21
00:01:40.953 --> 00:01:44.235
Which systems store transcripts or embeddings?

22
00:01:44.235 --> 00:01:46.877
Which logs include sensitive content?

23
00:01:46.877 --> 00:01:54.883
AI security architecture starts by making those paths explicit enough that teams can design controls intentionally.
